comments[17]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-29T22:37:34.363Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: hybrid expected-error diagnostics search plus complete PmCliErrorContext keyword search found existing host-specific contracts but no open extension-owned payload slot; checked live open and in-progress lists; related active provenance work retains its current scope."
  "2026-11-01T21:06:26.813Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (branch fix/refusal-contract-precision):\n\n- Contract: PmCliErrorContext gains an optional details slot typed PmCliErrorDetails (plain JSON: null, booleans, finite numbers, well-formed strings, arrays, plain objects). New core module src/core/shared/error-details.ts admits a value, returns a detached copy, and renders it whole within PM_CLI_ERROR_DETAILS_LIMITS (max_bytes 4096 of compact UTF-8 JSON, max_depth 8). Anything larger or malformed is replaced by a details_omitted receipt { reason, path?, byte_length?, byte_limit }. Partial structures are never emitted.\n- SDK: createPmCliExpectedError validates details at construction and throws a TypeError naming the reason and path (unsupported_value, non_finite_number, malformed_unicode, reserved_key for __proto__, depth_limit, not_plain_object). Size is data-dependent, so it is bounded at render time, not refused. Exports from sdk and sdk/runtime: PM_CLI_ERROR_DETAILS_LIMITS, isPmCliErrorDetails, PmCliErrorDetails, PmCliErrorDetailValue, PmCliErrorDetailsOmission, PmCliErrorDetailsRejection.\n- CLI: --json errors carry details or details_omitted (kept by --lean). Text output renders an indented TOON \"Details:\" block, which is the first collection the bounded text ladder drops. The telemetry classification is built without details.\n- MCP: errorContent and writeError now recognize separately bundled expected errors by shape (they previously forwarded context only for instanceof PmCliError) and bound details the same way.\n- Sentry: captured faults strip details from error_context, and beforeSend scrubs details from error-data contexts and extras.\n\nProof: tests/unit/core/shared/error-details.spec.ts (17 cases), error-guidance, MCP, Sentry helper/instrument and SDK index specs. tests/integration/telemetry/extension-error-provenance.spec.ts runs the built CLI under Node and Bun against real local OTLP and Sentry collectors: the probe extension's separately bundled refusal carries a marked details payload, the JSON refusal returns it whole, the text refusal renders the Details block, and the marker never appears in collected events, logs or telemetry.\n\nProducer adoption (pm-changelog selection_report) belongs in the pm-changelog repository once a pm-cli release ships this slot. Filed there as the follow-up after merge.\n"
  "2026-11-01T21:46:36.034Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1649 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh). Five findings on this item, all verified and fixed:\n- Nested Sentry payloads: console capture stores the error under extra.arguments and breadcrumbs, which the shallow scrub missed. omitEmbeddedPmCliErrorDetails now scrubs every embedded context/error_context in event extras, contexts and breadcrumbs (depth-bounded, cycle-safe, no getters run), in both beforeSend and beforeSendTransaction.\n- Fault message leaking producer keys: createPmCliExpectedError's TypeError now names only the reason; the path sits on a non-enumerable path property.\n- Amplification: shared array references could expand 10^8 leaves before the byte check. The walk now counts compact-JSON bytes as it goes and stops at the ceiling, so its cost is bounded by max_bytes (the test finishes in milliseconds). The receipt therefore no longer carries byte_length.\n- Unbounded omission paths: capped at 160 UTF-8 bytes without splitting a character.\n- Throwing details getters: accessors are refused without being run, at both the slot (CLI, MCP, telemetry omission) and inside values. Oversized well-formed details are kept as given and render as a byte_limit receipt.\nNew regression cases are in error-details, sdk-index, error-guidance, MCP and Sentry instrument specs.\n"
  "2026-11-01T22:35:04.360Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 on PR #1649: three findings on this item, all verified and fixed.\n- CLI enrichment ran a separately bundled error's details getter: buildPmCliRecoveryContext spread the raw context. It now attaches recovery to a descriptor-preserving copy (frozen contexts included), so the renderer reports the getter as unsupported_value without running it. Covered in-process in error-handler-projection.spec.ts with a frozen, getter-carrying context.\n- A root proxy whose getPrototypeOf trap throws escaped admission. The root shape check now runs inside the guarded walk.\n- The typed-refusal fallback headline (pm-btv6x7 path) cut by UTF-16 units and could split an emoji. buildFallbackTitleFromMessage now cuts by code points.\n"
  "2026-11-01T23:05:40.227Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review on PR #1649 (head f7e12f69b): three new findings on this item, all fixed.\n- Hostile proxy contexts and non-JSON members broke the MCP response. Foreign contexts are now read once as plain data by snapshotPmCliErrorContext (no getter runs, own enumerable data only, a details accessor becomes an unsupported_value receipt, an uninspectable context becomes {}), in both the CLI and MCP. MCP falls back to { code, context_unserializable: true } when JSON cannot encode the context.\n- Oversized details stayed a live reference, so mutating them after construction could change the refusal. createPmCliExpectedError now records the host-set receipt details_omitted: { reason: \"byte_limit\", byte_limit } in place of details. PmCliErrorContext declares details_omitted as a host-owned field; renderers re-validate it, and telemetry omission removes it too, since its path can echo producer keys.\nIts title-truncation, root-proxy and Unicode findings were already fixed in round 2.\n"
  "2026-11-01T23:44:15.375Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 on PR #1649: two findings on this item, both fixed.\n- MCP ran custom toJSON hooks after bounding, so a context whose toJSON returned oversized details bypassed the ceiling, and a serializer that throws on a second call broke the response. MCP now serializes the snapshot exactly once and projects details on the plain result. A non-object result falls back to context_unserializable.\n- Recorded details_omitted receipts were read through getters. Receipt fields are now read as own data descriptors, and accessor-backed fields are rejected without running.\n"
  "2026-11-02T00:01:12.933Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 6f3d87ab7 on PR #1649: createPmCliExpectedError with context: null threw from Object.hasOwn. null now means \"no context\" again, as it did before this change (regression case in sdk-index.spec.ts).\n"
  "2026-11-02T00:37:50.679Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR #1649: MCP serialized the context before bounding diagnostics, so a nested getter inside details ran and shared trees were expanded. MCP now admits details from the snapshot with the bounded, getter-free walk first, then serializes only the remaining host fields once. A context-level toJSON is ignored, so it can neither replace nor expand the diagnostics. Regression cases cover a nested details getter (an unsupported_value receipt, the getter never runs), shared amplifying references (a byte_limit receipt) and a context toJSON (never invoked).\n"
  "2026-11-02T01:12:35.058Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 on PR #1649: isPmCliExpectedError threw when inspecting a revoked-proxy context (Array.isArray throws), so MCP errorContent threw instead of writing an error envelope. The predicate is now total and returns false for anything it cannot inspect, which also protects the CLI and Sentry paths. Covered in sdk-index and MCP specs.\n"
  "2026-11-02T01:48:00.230Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 on PR #1649: omitPmCliErrorDetails and projectPmCliErrorContextDetails called Array.isArray outside their guards, so sentryCaptureCliError threw on a revoked-proxy context before captureException ran. Both checks now sit inside the guards, and the embedded-context scrubber skips nodes it cannot list. A captured fault with a revoked context reaches Sentry with error_context: {}.\n"
  "2026-11-02T02:25:41.880Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 7 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- Sentry's ExtraErrorData integration serializes an error's own context before beforeSend runs, so a context-level toJSON that copied details under another key (report.marker) survived the key-based scrub. Crash reports now carry an error context only through crashReportErrorContext: a getter-free snapshot reduced to top-level primitives, with no diagnostics, receipt or serializer. It is used for error_context and every embedded context, and beforeSend rebuilds the error-data entry from the original exception's own data property rather than from the serialized copy.\n- A 20M-character string was JSON-encoded (about 120 MB) before the ceiling refused it. Admission now checks a constant-time lower bound (UTF-8 JSON needs at least one byte per UTF-16 unit, plus quotes) for every string value and key before scanning, encoding or building a path. A 20M-character value or key is refused in milliseconds.\n"
  "2026-11-02T03:03:36.104Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 8 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- Console-captured errors: Sentry normalizes console arguments and breadcrumbs (running custom toJSON hooks) before beforeSend, so a toJSON that flattened a diagnostic into a top-level primitive survived the primitive-only projection. Serialized embedded contexts whose originals cannot be recovered are now discarded (replaced with {}). Error-data entries are still rebuilt from the original exception, and the error_context extra our own capture produced is kept as its safe projection.\n- A proxied diagnostics array could report a different length on each read (1, then NaN), which turned the byte count into NaN and disabled the ceiling. The length is now read once from its own descriptor and validated as a non-negative safe integer, and the ceiling check treats a non-finite count as exceeded.\n"
  "2026-11-02T03:44:09.339Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 9 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed.\n- A console-captured context whose toJSON returns a string was normalized into a string, which the object-only discard kept. Embedded context and error_context slots are now discarded whatever their serialized type, then only independently verified safe projections are restored (rebuilt error-data entries and our own error_context).\n- Admitted details fit their 4,096-byte compact ceiling but can grow several times larger pretty-printed, so the generic diagnostic ladder dropped them from --json with only a generic omission entry. When details would push the pretty-printed envelope past the error diagnostic budget, they are now replaced up front with details_omitted: { reason: \"diagnostic_budget\", byte_limit }. PmCliErrorDetailsRejection gains that reason; the type is unreleased, and the SDK surface change is acknowledged.\n"
  "2026-11-02T04:18:03.258Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 10 on PR #1649: two findings, both fixed, plus one the new tests surfaced.\n- An error-level toJSON makes Sentry's ExtraErrorData integration emit siblings of context (contexts.Error.report) that carried the diagnostic, and only .context was being sanitized. Every entry the integration keyed by the exception's name or constructor name, and every entry carrying a context, is now replaced whole by a projection rebuilt from the original: its own top-level primitive fields plus a safe context. An uninspectable original yields {}.\n- A diagnostic proxy whose trap throws a revoked proxy made the instanceof DetailsShapeError classification itself throw. Fault classification is now guarded and defaults to unsupported_value.\n- Surfaced by the new tests: isPmCliExpectedError read error.name before its guard, so a throwing name getter escaped the Sentry hook. The whole predicate is now total.\n"
  "2026-11-02T04:54:37.949Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 11 on PR #1649: two findings on this item, both fixed.\n- An error's own toJSON can move diagnostics out of every named context slot, so a console.error(error) argument serialized to { report: ... } survived. Complete serialized console arguments (extra.arguments and breadcrumb data.arguments) are now discarded as empty arrays. The SDK runtime's extension-activation fallback, the one host console.error that passed an error object, now logs the message only.\n- projectPmCliErrorContextDetails, the public custom-host projection, kept a context-level toJSON, so serializing the projection could re-emit oversized diagnostics. Both it and the telemetry omission now drop toJSON, as the MCP boundary already did.\n"
  "2026-11-02T05:32:52.930Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 12 on PR #1649: the custom-host projection and the telemetry omission returned a context unchanged when it had neither details nor a receipt, even if it carried its own toJSON that could emit oversized diagnostics. A context-level serializer alone now triggers the projection, which drops it.\n"
  "2026-11-02T06:09:24.557Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 13 on PR #1649: a context class with an inherited (prototype) toJSON bypassed the own-key fast path, so projectPmCliErrorContextDetails and omitPmCliErrorDetails returned the original object with its serializer. Both now always return a plain own-data copy, so no serializer, own or inherited, survives projection.\n"
